true, true
),
(
'choosing-cuticle-nipper-jaw-size',
'Quarter, Half, or Full Jaw: Choosing Cuticle Nipper Size',
'Jaw size is a tool-selection decision, not a preference — what a quarter, half, and full jaw each do well, and why most techs need two.',
'Nail & Cuticle',
$g$Jaw size means the length of the cutting edge, expressed as a fraction of a notional full jaw. There is no industry standard for the naming, so the same nipper can be sold as a "half jaw", a "jaw 14", or by a millimetre figure. Read the measurement, not the label.

Quarter jaw — roughly four to five millimetres of edge. Precision work: sidewalls, tight corners, thin or delicate cuticle, and tidying around a nail bed without catching surrounding skin. Slow on volume, unmatched on control.

Half jaw — roughly seven to eight millimetres. The professional default, and the right single nipper if you only buy one. Enough edge to clear a cuticle efficiently, short enough to keep tip control near the sidewall.

Full jaw — roughly twelve to fourteen millimetres. Built for thick, overgrown, or calloused cuticle, mostly in pedicure work. On a fine manicure it removes more than intended and is hard to steer.

The common mistake is one nipper for everything. A half jaw asked to handle heavy pedicure cuticle takes four passes; a full jaw asked to work a delicate sidewall lifts and tears the eponychium. A half and a full jaw between them cover almost all professional work.

Match handle length to your grip, too. Longer handles give leverage for dense tissue; shorter handles sit closer to the fingertips for detail work. Suppliers rarely list handle length, so ask.

See also: spring design affects how a nipper feels long before the edge dulls — see [Single-Spring vs. Double-Spring Nippers](/guides/single-spring-vs-double-spring-nippers).$g$,
'[{"q":"What jaw size should a new nail technician buy first?","a":"A half jaw. It is the professional default and handles the majority of manicure and pedicure cuticle work; add a full jaw once you take on heavier pedicure clients."},{"q":"Are jaw size names standardised across brands?","a":"No. Quarter, half, and full are approximate, and trade numbers like jaw 7, 14, and 16 vary by supplier. Buy on the stated cutting-edge measurement where one is given."}]'::jsonb,
false, true
),
(
'single-spring-vs-double-spring-nippers',
'Single-Spring vs. Double-Spring Nippers: Which Lasts Longer?',
'The spring returns the handles — and decides how the tool feels after five hundred cuts. Barrel, single leaf, and double leaf compared.',
'Nail & Cuticle',
$g$The spring in a nipper does one job: it returns the handles to the open position after each cut. It does not affect sharpness. It does decide how tiring the tool is over a full day, and how gracefully it ages.

Single-leaf springs are the simplest and the cheapest. Light action out of the box, and easy to replace or re-tension. They also lose tension first — after a few hundred cycles the handles stop springing fully open and the tech starts opening them by hand without noticing.

Double-leaf springs distribute load across two arms. The action is firmer at the start, which some techs read as stiff, but tension holds far longer and the return stays symmetrical. For a high-volume salon nipper this is usually the better buy.

Barrel springs sit inside the handles rather than between them. Smoothest feel of the three and nothing exposed to snag, but if a barrel spring fails it is generally not field-serviceable.

Judge the joint alongside the spring. A box joint — where the two halves pass through each other — keeps the jaws aligned under load far better than a lap joint pinned on the outside. Gummy action and jaws that no longer meet cleanly are usually a joint problem, not a spring problem.

Whatever the design, tension is not the failure mode you should be planning around — moisture in the joint is. Dry and oil the pivot after every reprocessing cycle and most nippers outlive their springs.

See also: sizing the cutting edge to the work is covered in [Quarter, Half, or Full Jaw](/guides/choosing-cuticle-nipper-jaw-size).$g$,
