'traveling-with-professional-tools-tsa',
'Traveling With Professional Tools: TSA Rules That Actually Apply',
'What the current TSA guidance says about shears, tweezers, nippers, and clippers — and how to pack a working kit for a flight.',
'Kits',
$g$Scissors and shears are the item people get wrong. TSA allows scissors in carry-on only when the blades measure less than four inches from the pivot point. Most professional barber shears are five to seven inches overall and will not clear that line — they go in checked baggage, sheathed.

Tweezers are permitted in carry-on and checked bags with no length restriction, which makes a lash kit far easier to travel with than a barber kit. Tip guards still matter, for the tips rather than for security.

Nail clippers and cuticle nippers are allowed in carry-on. Sharp implements in checked bags must be sheathed or securely wrapped so a baggage handler or inspector is not cut opening the case.

Razors follow their own rule set: disposable and cartridge razors are fine in carry-on, but straight razors and loose blades are checked-bag only.

The officer at the checkpoint has the final call on any item, whatever the published list says. Building a kit that assumes the strictest reading — shears checked, everything else carried — means a surprise at the lane costs you nothing.

Practical packing: put the irreplaceable, awkward-to-rebuy items in your carry-on and the restricted ones in a hard case in checked baggage, with a printed inventory. Declare nothing you do not need to, but be ready to open the case.

Rules change. Confirm the current entry on the TSA "What Can I Bring?" list for each item before you fly, and check the equivalent authority for international departures.

See also: building the case itself is covered in [What Goes in a Professional Grooming Kit](/guides/what-goes-in-a-professional-grooming-kit).$g$,
'[{"q":"Can I bring barber shears in my carry-on?","a":"Only if the blades are under four inches measured from the pivot point. Most professional shears exceed that, so they belong in checked baggage, sheathed."},{"q":"Are lash tweezers allowed through security?","a":"Yes — tweezers are permitted in both carry-on and checked bags. Use tip guards to protect the points in transit."},{"q":"Do cuticle nippers count as a prohibited sharp object?","a":"No. Nail clippers and cuticle nippers are permitted in carry-on; anything sharp in checked baggage should be sheathed or wrapped."}]'::jsonb,
false, true
),
(
'what-goes-in-a-professional-grooming-kit',
'What Goes in a Professional Grooming Kit',
'A build list by discipline — the implements that earn their space, and the ones that only look essential.',
'Kits',
$g$Start from the services you actually sell. A kit assembled from a supplier''s "complete set" carries pieces you will never open and misses the second pair of the tool you use hourly. Build the list from your service menu backwards.

Core across every discipline: two of whatever you use most. A single pair of anything means a dropped or soiled tool ends the service. Duplicates of the primary implement, singles of the rest, is the ratio that works.

Lash and brow: a straight isolation tweezer, a curved or boot-tip volume tweezer, tip guards, and a lint-free wipe supply. Grip and tip geometry matter more than count — see [Isolation vs. Volume Lash Tweezers](/guides/isolation-vs-volume-lash-tweezers).

Nail and cuticle: a half-jaw nipper for general work, a full jaw if you take pedicure clients, a cuticle pusher, and a glass or metal file that can be disinfected. Sizing is covered in [Quarter, Half, or Full Jaw](/guides/choosing-cuticle-nipper-jaw-size).

Barber and hair: a primary cutting shear, a thinning or texturizing shear, a sheath for each, and a small oil bottle. Whether you genuinely need both shears is argued out in [Thinning/Texturizing Shears vs. Cutting Shears](/guides/thinning-shears-vs-cutting-shears).

Reprocessing supplies count as kit. Detergent and brush, an EPA-registered hospital-grade disinfectant with a dated container, clean toweling, and two labelled containers for clean and soiled implements. Without these the rest of the kit cannot legally be reused between clients.

What to leave out: single-use novelties, tools duplicating a service you do not offer, and anything porous that cannot be disinfected.

See also: [How to Store and Organize a Professional Tool Kit](/guides/store-organize-professional-tool-kit) and [Traveling With Professional Tools](/guides/traveling-with-professional-tools-tsa).$g$,
null,
false, true
